          "type": "mc",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "timeLimit": 60,
          "stem": "The graphs of \\(y=ax\\) and \\(y=\\tan^{-1}(bx)\\) intersect at three distinct points if:",
          "hint": "Compare the gradient of tan⁻¹(bx) at the origin (= b) with the gradient of the line (= a).",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Both graphs pass through the origin, and both are odd functions, so other intersections come in a pair \\(\\pm x_0\\).",
              "work": "\\(x=0\\ \\text{is always an intersection}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Gradient of \\(y=\\tan^{-1}(bx)\\) at the origin:",
              "work": "\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b}{1+b^2x^2}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{at }x=0:\\ b\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "\\(\\tan^{-1}(bx)\\) flattens out towards \\(\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), while the line keeps going. So there are two extra crossings only if the curve leaves the origin more steeply than the line, in the same direction: \\(b\\) and \\(a\\) have the same sign and \\(|b|\\gt|a|\\).",
              "work": "\\(b\\gt a\\gt 0\\ \\ \\text{or}\\ \\ b\\lt a\\lt 0\\)",
