              "timeLimit": 60,
              "stem": "The graph of the function \\(y=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right)\\) is transformed by a translation left by 1 unit, then a horizontal dilation with a scale factor 2. Find the equation of the transformed graph.",
              "hint": "For a left translation by 1, substitute \\(x+1\\); for a horizontal dilation by 2, then substitute \\(x/2\\). Keep the whole input to \\(\\tan^{-1}\\) grouped.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "explain": "Translating the graph left by 1 replaces the input \\(x\\) by \\(x+1\\).",
                  "work": "\\(y=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{(x+1)-2}{2}\\right)=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x-1}{2}\\right)\\)"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "A horizontal dilation by scale factor 2 replaces the input in the translated equation by \\(\\frac{x}{2}\\).",
                  "work": "\\(y=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{(x/2)-1}{2}\\right)\\)"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "Simplify the argument before identifying the transformed equation.",
                  "work": "\\(\\frac{x/2-1}{2}=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac12=\\frac{x-2}{4}\\Rightarrow y=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)\\)"
                },
                {
                  "explain": "This is option D. The order matters because the translation is applied before the dilation.",
                  "work": "\\(\\boxed{y=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)}\\)"
                }
